# Siple configuration reference

Siple is configured by one TOML file, `config.toml`. Every setting is a key in it, and changes apply while Siple runs. The
Settings window and the control API only write that file. Unknown keys are errors, so a typo is caught.

- Print a commented example with `siple-cli config-example`. It is also written to your config folder on first run (mode 0600,
  because it can hold passwords).
- The schema is `crates/siple-config/src/schema.rs`. `state.toml` holds app state that is not a setting.
- New to this? Start with the [user guide](https://zyno-io.github.io/siple/guide/configuration).

Each table shows the key, its **default**, and what it does. Keys apply at once unless a section or row says otherwise (see
[Reload classes](#reload-classes)).

## Location

| OS | Default config file |
|---|---|
| macOS | `~/Library/Application Support/Siple/config.toml` |
| Windows | `%APPDATA%\Siple\config.toml` |
| Linux | `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/siple/config.toml` |

Store editions keep their data where the sandbox or package puts it:

| Edition | Config folder |
|---|---|
| Mac App Store | `~/Library/Group Containers/VUDU4KEQ99.io.zyno.siple/Siple/` |
| Microsoft Store | `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Packages\<package family name>\LocalState\Siple\` |
| Flatpak | `~/.var/app/io.zyno.siple/config/siple/` |

The AppImage uses the Linux default. `status.build.channel` (`direct`, `appimage`, `mas`, `msix`, `flatpak`) tells you which
edition runs.

To use another file, pass `--config PATH` (any binary or subcommand) or set `SIPLE_CONFIG`. The lock file, `runtime.json`,
`state.toml`, the control socket and the logs live next to the config file, so each config folder is its own instance.
Relative paths in the config (TLS files, `file:` and `record:` audio, `log_dir`) are relative to that folder.

<details>
<summary>Notes per edition</summary>

- **Mac App Store:** the control socket is `control.sock` in the config folder, or `s.sock` in the group container if that path
  is too long. Set `[control] tcp_listen` if even that is too long.
- **Flatpak:** the socket fallback for a long path is `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/app/io.zyno.siple/`. The sandbox sees only
  `~/.var/app/io.zyno.siple/`. Keep TLS files and `file:` audio there, or run
  `flatpak override --user --filesystem=/path:ro io.zyno.siple`.
- **AppImage:** portable mode (`<file>.config` next to the file, or `--appimage-portable-config`) moves the config folder.

</details>

## Reloading

Siple watches the config folder and applies edits after a short pause. To force a reload, run `siple-cli ctl config.reload`
(or send SIGHUP on macOS). It returns what changed.

- A bad file never replaces the last good config. Siple logs the error, shows a banner and keeps the old settings.
- Runtime overrides (`account.set_dnd`, `account.set_sip_trace`) last until that account's same field changes in the file.
- Accounts are matched by `id` (case-insensitive). A new id starts an account. A removed id, or `enabled = false`, unregisters it and hangs up its
  calls. Changing an `id` is a remove plus an add.

### Reload classes

| Class | What happens |
|---|---|
| **hot** | Applied at once. Calls are untouched. |
| **next call** | Stored now. Later calls use it. |
| **re-register** | A fresh REGISTER. Changing `username` or `domain` unregisters the old address first. |
| **rebuild** | The account's transport is rebuilt. It waits until the account has no calls, unless `[general] reconfig_force = true`. |
| **rebind** | `[control]` listeners restart. Only changed endpoints are replaced. |
| **restart** | Takes effect at the next start of Siple. |

## Editing from the GUI and the API

Settings and the `config.edit` API method change the file through one writer. A write fails on a stale `base_hash`, refuses
a file that does not parse, changes only the named keys (comments, order and quoting stay), re-validates, replaces the file
atomically and reloads. A key you did not set, whose new value equals its default, is not added. Saving from Settings writes a value the vendor profile
supplied, which pins it. Passwords and the control token are never returned.

File-only keys: `log_dir`, `log_retention_days` and `reconfig_force` in `[general]`, all of `[control]`, `[media]` and `[speech]`,
and account keys the window has no field for (such as `bind_address`, `tls_ca_file` and `extra_headers`).
Details: [user guide](https://zyno-io.github.io/siple/guide/configuration#editing-the-file-from-the-window-or-the-api) and
[API reference](API.md#configedit). An older Siple rejects a file with keys it does not know.

## `[general]`

| Key | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `user_agent` | `"Siple/<version>"` | Default `User-Agent` header. |
| `log_level` | `"info"` | A level (trace, debug, info, warn, error, off) or filter such as `info,siple_sip=debug`. |
| `log_dir` | `""` | Log folder. `""` is `<config folder>/logs`. Restart. |
| `log_retention_days` | `14` | Delete older logs at startup. `0` keeps all. Restart. |
| `reconfig_force` | `false` | Rebuild transports on config change even during calls, which are hung up. |

## `[logging]`

| Key | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `redact_srtp_keys` | `false` | Mask SRTP master keys in the log and SIP traces. |
| `redact_credentials` | `false` | Mask digest secrets, the control token and passwords (4+ characters) in the log and traces. |

### Logs and secrets

Both redaction options are off by default, and the per-account SIP trace (`sip_trace`) is on. Turn redaction on before you share
a log or trace. Siple never writes a plaintext password to a log itself, and it has no telemetry. Its only own network activity
is the optional [update check](#updates). How to share logs safely:
[Troubleshooting](https://zyno-io.github.io/siple/guide/troubleshooting#share-a-log-safely).

```toml
[logging]
redact_credentials = true
redact_srtp_keys = true
```

## `[control]`

Where the control API listens. Changes **rebind**.

| Key | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `unix_socket` | `""` | Unix socket (macOS, Linux). `""` is `<config folder>/control.sock`, or a short fallback if that is too long. |
| `named_pipe` | `""` | Windows pipe, starting with `\\.\pipe\`. `""` is `\\.\pipe\siple-<user>-<hash>`. |
| `tcp_listen` | `""` | Loopback TCP address such as `"127.0.0.1:5079"`. `""` is off. The token is always required. |

## `[network]`

Siple re-probes its route and re-registers when your network changes. With no route, an account shows `waiting_for_network`. See
[Network changes](https://zyno-io.github.io/siple/guide/network).

| Key | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `watch` | `true` | Watch OS network changes and sleep/wake. `false` leaves only the periodic probe. |
| `probe_secs` | `30` | Re-probe every N seconds. `0` is events only; otherwise 5 to 3600. |
| `debounce_ms` | `1500` | Batch change triggers for this long. 100 to 60000. |
| `move_calls` | `true` | Keep calls across a change by moving them. `false` ends them with `network_lost`. |

## `[media]`

Shared by all accounts. Changes apply to the **next call** (DTMF keys also to calls in progress).

| Key | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `rtp_port_range` | `[16384, 32767]` | RTP/RTCP ports. Start is even and at least 1024; end is above start. Open it in your firewall. |
| `codecs` | `["opus", "g722", "pcmu", "pcma"]` | Codecs in preference order. At least one, no duplicates. |
| `codec_answer_order` | `"offer"` | `offer`: caller's order. `local`: our order. |
| `jitter_min_ms` | `40` | Jitter buffer minimum. Not above the maximum. |
| `jitter_max_ms` | `200` | Jitter buffer maximum. 1 to 1000. |
| `comfort_noise` | `false` | Use comfort noise (RFC 3389) if the peer offers it. |
| `symmetric_rtp` | `false` | Reply to where audio came from. Helps behind NAT. |
| `rtcp_mux` | `false` | Offer `a=rtcp-mux`. |
| `rx_history_secs` | `60` | Received audio kept per call for `call.listen`. 5 to 600. |
| `dtmf_mode` | `"auto"` | `rfc4733`, `info`, `inband` or `auto`. |
| `dtmf_duration_ms` | `100` | Length of a sent digit. 10 to 10000. |
| `dtmf_gap_ms` | `70` | Silence between digits. 0 to 5000. |

`rx_history_secs` uses about 32 KB per second per call. `auto` DTMF uses RFC 4733 if negotiated, else SIP INFO.

## `[audio]`

Which sound devices to use. Device names must match what `audio.devices` lists. If a device is unplugged, Siple uses the system
default until it returns. Settings and `audio.set_devices` write this section.

| Key | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `backend` | `"auto"` | `auto`, `none` (never open sound devices) or `always`. Restart. |
| `input` | `""` | Microphone. `""` is the system default. |
| `output` | `""` | Speaker. `""` is the system default. |
| `ringer` | `""` | Ringer. `""` is the same as `output`. |

`none` suits servers and containers (`silence`, `tone:` and `file:` audio still work). `--no-audio` forces it. On macOS and
Windows `auto` is `always`. On Linux, `auto` checks once at startup and turns audio off, with an INFO line
(`status.audio.disabled_reason`), unless it finds a sound device or server. A headset plugged in later, or PulseAudio configured only through `client.conf`, is not seen: set
`always`. The device list hides ALSA pseudo-devices (`null`, `dmix`, `dsnoop`, `surround*`, `iec958`, `front`, plugins). alsa-lib's
messages go to the log at DEBUG (target `alsa`).

## `[calls]`

Behavior shared by all accounts.

| Key | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `decline_code` | `486` | SIP status that Reject sends while another call is live. 400 to 699. |
| `hold_scope` | `"all"` | Auto-hold scope: `all` (every account) or `account` (only the account that answers or dials). |
| `resume_after_failed_transfer` | `true` | Take the original call off hold when a transfer fails for good. |

Use `hold_scope = "account"` for automation, or for two local accounts that call each other. Auto-hold skips calls that use no
audio device and the other leg of a call between two local accounts. A transfer with no result after 32 seconds is never
resumed automatically. An explicit `call.activate`, or `call.answer` with `mode = "hold_others"`, ignores `auto_hold`.

## `[ui]`

Desktop window settings. All keys are hot. Settings writes this table. Guides:
[tray and startup](https://zyno-io.github.io/siple/guide/tray), [phone links](https://zyno-io.github.io/siple/guide/links),
[extensions](https://zyno-io.github.io/siple/guide/extensions).

### Look and layout

| Key | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `theme` | `"system"` | `system`, `light` or `dark`. |
| `density` | `"comfortable"` | `comfortable` or `compact`. |
| `reduced_motion` | `"system"` | `system`, `on` or `off`. |
| `blf_view` | `"keys"` | Extension layout: `keys`, `lamps` or `list`. |
| `blf_group_by` | `"account"` | Group extensions by `account`, `group` or `none`. |
| `blf_click` | `"dial"` | Click an idle extension: `dial` or `prefill`. |
| `blf_click_in_call` | `"menu"` | Click an extension during a call: `menu`, `consult` or `blind`. |
| `blf_click_ringing` | `"pickup"` | Click a ringing extension: `pickup` or `call`. |
| `transfer_enter` | `"consult"` | Enter in the transfer box: `consult` or `blind`. |
| `developer_tools` | `false` | Show the Log pane, Open logs and Open config, and registration times. The API and MCP stay on either way. |

### Tray, window and startup

| Key | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `tray` | `true` | Show a tray or menu-bar icon. `false` makes closing the window quit. |
| `close_to_tray` | `true` | Closing the window hides it. Quit from the tray menu. |
| `hide_from_taskbar` | `true` | No Dock icon or taskbar button while hidden. |
| `start_hidden` | `false` | Start with the window hidden. |
| `show_on_incoming` | `true` | Raise the window on an incoming call. |
| `show_on_answer` | `false` | Raise the window when a call is answered. |
| `notifications` | `true` | Desktop notifications for calls and transfers. |
| `launch_at_login` | `false` | Start Siple when you log in. Pair with `start_hidden`. |
| `handle_links` | `true` | Open `sip:`, `sips:`, `tel:`, `callto:` and `siple:` links. |

Links fill the dial field and never dial. A `siple:` setup link changes nothing until you review and confirm it
([setup links](https://zyno-io.github.io/siple/guide/setup-links)). Quit also works with Cmd+Q (macOS), Ctrl+Q (Windows, Linux)
or SIGTERM. An update restart ignores `start_hidden` and restores the window as it was. Notifications on macOS need the signed
app. `launch_at_login` is also in Settings > Notifications > Tray and window and in the first-run wizard.

<details>
<summary>Platform and edition notes</summary>

- **Linux tray:** a StatusNotifierItem (KDE, XFCE, GNOME with the AppIndicator extension). Without a tray host, closing
  minimizes. On Wayland the window cannot be hidden, so closing minimizes and `start_hidden` has no effect. For full
  hide-to-tray run `env -u WAYLAND_DISPLAY -u WAYLAND_SOCKET siple`.
- **`launch_at_login`** writes a macOS LaunchAgent, a Windows `HKCU\...\Run` entry or a Linux autostart `.desktop` file for the
  running executable (the `.AppImage` file in the AppImage edition). `false` removes it. A non-default `--config` profile has its
  own entry.
  - *Mac App Store:* uses `SMAppService` (System Settings > General > Login Items). The switch there wins, and a non-default
    `--config` profile leaves it alone.
  - *Microsoft Store:* no effect (logged at info), and Settings shows a note. Use Windows Settings > Apps > Startup.
  - *Flatpak:* goes through the desktop's Background portal, which may ask once. One entry serves the app, so the last profile
    to enable it wins. `false` removes the entry only if this profile registered it.
- **`handle_links`** changes things at runtime only on Windows: Siple registers for `sip`, `sips`, `tel` and `callto` (pick it in
  Settings > Default apps) and for `siple`, and `false` removes both. Other platforms and all packaged editions register links at
  install time, so the key does nothing there.
- **API:** `ui.show`, `ui.hide`, `ui.state`, `ui.attention` and `app.open_uri` control the window.

</details>

### `[ui.shortcuts]`

Maps an action to a key chord, such as `answer = "Mod+Shift+A"`. Actions you leave out keep their defaults. An unknown action or
a malformed chord is an error. `Mod` is Cmd on macOS and Ctrl elsewhere.

| Action | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `answer` | `Mod+Shift+A` | Answer the selected ringing call, else the newest. |
| `hangup` | `Mod+Shift+E` | Hang up, cancel a dialing call, or decline a ringing one. |
| `mute` | `Mod+Shift+M` | Mute or unmute. |
| `hold` | `Mod+Shift+H` | Hold or resume. |
| `transfer` | `Mod+Shift+T` | Open the transfer box. |
| `line_1` to `line_9` | `Mod+1` to `Mod+9` | Select that line. |
| `line_prev`, `line_next` | `Mod+[`, `Mod+]` | Select the previous or next line. |
| `focus_dial` | `Mod+L` | Focus the dial field. |
| `filter_extensions` | `/` | Focus the extensions filter. |
| `settings` | `Mod+,` | Open or close Settings. |

<details>
<summary>Chord syntax and rules</summary>

A chord is modifiers and one key joined by `+`, case-insensitive: `Mod+Shift+A`, `Mod+,`, `Alt+F4`, `/`.

- **Modifiers:** `Mod`, `Ctrl`, `Alt` (Option), `Shift`, `Cmd` (the Command key itself; it never fires off macOS).
- **Keys:** a letter or digit, one of `` , . / ; ' [ ] \ - = ` ``, `Plus`, `Enter`, `Tab`, `Space`, `Escape`, `Backspace`, `Delete`,
  `Insert`, `Home`, `End`, `PageUp`, `PageDown`, `Up`, `Down`, `Left`, `Right` or `F1` to `F12`.
- Shift and Alt must match exactly, except on punctuation keys such as `/`, where Shift is ignored unless named. Digit and bracket
  chords also match the physical key.
- **Errors:** `Escape` as the key, a digit with no Mod, Ctrl, Cmd or Alt, `Mod+C`, `Mod+V`, `Mod+X`, and two actions with the same
  effective chord.
- Remove a bad entry with `config.edit` `reset_section ui shortcuts.<name>`. Settings > Shortcuts lists problems.

Digits, `*` and `#` send DTMF while a line is active and no text field has focus. System-wide hotkeys are not available.

</details>

## `[speech]`

Optional external text-to-speech and speech-to-text programs, used by `call.say`, `call.listen{transcribe}` and `speech.*`. Off by
default, nothing is bundled. The Mac App Store and Flatpak editions cannot run external commands: the section is accepted with a
log warning, the methods fail with `unsupported`, and Settings says "not available in this edition".

| Key | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `tts_command` | `[]` | TTS program and arguments. `[]` is off. |
| `tts_timeout_ms` | `30000` | Kill the program after this long. 1000 to 120000. |
| `tts_voice` | `""` | Default for `{voice}`. |
| `stt_command` | `[]` | STT program and arguments. `[]` is off. |
| `stt_timeout_ms` | `60000` | Kill the program after this long. 1000 to 120000. |
| `stt_language` | `"en"` | Default for `{lang}`. |

Placeholders (inside a single argument): TTS has `{text_file}` (UTF-8 text), `{out}` (WAV to write) and `{voice}`. STT has `{in}`
(16 kHz mono WAV), `{lang}`, `{out_base}` and `{out_txt}`. Without `{out}` or `{out_txt}`, Siple reads stdout. `{{` and `}}` give
literal braces, and an unknown placeholder is an error. No shell is involved. Voice and language values match
`[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_.+:-]{0,63}`. The text to speak goes on stdin and in `{text_file}`, never in an argument.

## `[updates]`

Siple tells you when a newer release exists. Direct downloads can also install it in place when you choose. The store editions
open their store page. All keys are hot. Guide: [Updates](https://zyno-io.github.io/siple/guide/updates).

| Key | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `check` | `true` | Check for updates. `false` turns off all update network activity and installs. |
| `install` | `true` | Direct downloads: allow the in-place install. `false` opens the release page and stops an install that has not replaced files yet. |
| `channel` | unset | Direct downloads: `stable` or `prerelease`. Unset follows the build you run. |

A check runs about 30 seconds after start, then hourly (direct) or daily (stores). Unset `channel` offers release candidates only
while you run one. `reset_section updates channel` returns to unset.

### What an update check sends

Each request carries `User-Agent: Siple/<version>` and an `Accept` header, plus `If-None-Match` (the previous ETag) to siple.app.
No identifier, account, usage data or cookies. Each host sees your IP address.

| Edition | Request |
|---|---|
| Direct download | `https://siple.app/updates/v1/releases.json` about hourly. GitHub's releases API is the fallback when siple.app fails, or a cross-check when it answers. GitHub is asked at most once per 24 hours in total. |
| Mac App Store | `https://itunes.apple.com/lookup?id=6836164262` about daily. TestFlight sends nothing. |
| Microsoft Store | No request from Siple. Windows' Store service is asked whether an update is waiting. |
| Flatpak | Nothing. Flathub updates the app. |

### In-place install (direct downloads)

Only when you choose it, and only with `check` and `install` on. Siple downloads the signed checksum list and one archive from the
project's GitHub release, verifies the signature, checksum, layout and publisher, and restarts when no call is active. Your
config, accounts, logs and keychain entries are never touched. If any check fails, nothing changes and the pill opens the release
page. It is also unavailable for package-manager installs, unwritable folders, containers, headless runs and development builds.
The stores' editions answer `app.install_update` with `unsupported`. Full steps:
[user guide](https://zyno-io.github.io/siple/guide/updates#what-is-checked-before-an-install-replaces-anything).

API: `app.update_status`, `app.check_updates`, `app.install_update`, `app.cancel_update`, and the events `app.update_available`,
`app.update_progress` and `app.updated`.

## `[[account]]`

Repeat the table for each account. You need only `id`, `username` and `domain`. The shipped example has `enabled = false`, so a
fresh install never tries to register. Keys apply at once unless the description says
"(re-registers)", "(rebuilds)" or "(next call)" (see [Reload classes](#reload-classes)).

### Identity and registration

| Key | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | required | Unique name, `[A-Za-z0-9_.-]`. Used in the API and file names. |
| `enabled` | `true` | `false` unregisters and hangs up at once. Turning on rebuilds. |
| `profile` | `"generic"` | Vendor preset. See [Vendor profiles](#vendor-profiles). |
| `display_name` | `""` | Name in the From header. |
| `username` | required | User part of your address. No spaces or `@` (re-registers) |
| `auth_username` | `""` | Login name if different. `""` is `username` (re-registers) |
| `password` | `""` | Login password. Hidden in `config.get` and logs (re-registers) |
| `password_store` | `"file"` | `file` or `keyring` ([below](#passwords-in-the-system-keyring)) (re-registers) |
| `domain` | required | Domain of your address. A bare host (re-registers) |
| `registrar` | `""` | Server to send requests to, `host[:port]`. `""` is `domain` (rebuilds) |
| `register` | `true` | `false` is peer mode: no REGISTER, calls still work (re-registers) |
| `expires` | `300` | Registration lifetime in seconds. 10 to 86400 (re-registers) |
| `user_agent` | `""` | Overrides `[general].user_agent`. |
| `extra_headers` | `{}` | Headers added to requests, such as `{ "X-Test" = "1" }`. |

`password` must be empty with `password_store = "keyring"`. If `registrar` is empty, a `domain` change also moves the next hop and
is a rebuild. Reserved header names for `extra_headers`: `Via`, `From`, `To`, `Call-ID`, `CSeq`, `Contact`, `Content-Length`.

### Transport, NAT and keep-alive

| Key | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `transport` | `"udp"` | `udp`, `tcp`, `tls` or `auto` (rebuilds) |
| `local_port` | `0` | Local SIP port. `0` is any free port (rebuilds) |
| `bind_address` | `""` | Local IP to bind and advertise. `""` is automatic (rebuilds) |
| `public_address` | `""` | IP or host to advertise (static NAT) (rebuilds) |
| `rewrite_contact` | `true` | Learn your public address from the server and re-register with it (re-registers) |
| `outbound_proxy` | `""` | Send every request through this `host[:port]` (rebuilds) |
| `outbound_proxy_route` | `false` | Also add a `Route` header for the proxy (rebuilds) |
| `use_sips_uri` | `false` | On TLS, use a `sips:` Contact (rebuilds) |
| `keepalive_secs` | `30` | NAT keep-alive interval. `0` is off; otherwise 5 to 3600. |
| `keepalive_method` | `"crlf"` | `crlf` or `options`. |
| `keepalive_fail_threshold` | `2` | Unanswered `options` keep-alives that mark the connection dead. `0` is never; max 20. |

- `auto` looks up DNS SRV records for the next hop: `_sips._tcp` (TLS), then `_sip._tcp`, then `_sip._udp`. With no SRV record, Siple uses UDP on port 5060. An IP
  address or an explicit `host:port` skips SRV and uses UDP on that port. [Details](https://zyno-io.github.io/siple/guide/network).
- Two enabled accounts cannot share a non-zero `local_port`. `bind_address` must be an IP.
- An explicit `public_address` turns off Contact rewriting.
- `options` also detects dead connections: any SIP response counts as alive.

### TLS

Used when the transport is TLS (with `auto`, only if TLS is picked). Changes rebuild the transport.

| Key | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `tls_verify` | `true` | Verify the server certificate. `false` ignores errors (test PBXs only). |
| `tls_ca_file` | `""` | PEM file with extra trusted CAs. |
| `tls_server_name` | `""` | Override the name used for SNI and verification. |
| `tls_fingerprint` | `""` | Pin the server certificate: SHA-256 as 64 hex digits (colons optional). |
| `tls_cert`, `tls_key` | `""` | PEM certificate and key for the listener or client auth. Set both or neither. |

An untrusted certificate shows `tls_untrusted`, and `account.test` reports its fingerprint. A pinned certificate is accepted
whatever its chain, expiry or name, and `tls_verify` and `tls_ca_file` are ignored. Get one with
`openssl x509 -in pbx.pem -noout -fingerprint -sha256`. Empty `tls_cert` means self-signed. A missing TLS file is a warning.

### Media and SRTP

Changes apply to the next call.

| Key | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `srtp` | `"optional"` | `off`, `optional` or `mandatory`. |
| `srtp_suites` | `["AES_CM_128_HMAC_SHA1_80", "AES_CM_128_HMAC_SHA1_32"]` | Accepted SRTP suites, in order. |
| `codecs` | `[]` | Codec order. `[]` inherits `[media].codecs`. |
| `dtmf_mode` | `""` | `rfc4733`, `info`, `inband` or `auto`. `""` inherits `[media]`. |
| `audio_source` | `"device"` | Audio sent: `device`, `silence`, `tone:<hz>` or `file:<path.wav>` (looped). |
| `audio_sink` | `"device"` | Audio received: `device`, `null` or `record:<path.wav>`. |

- `off` sends plain RTP only. `optional` offers SRTP and uses it if the peer agrees. If the PBX refuses (488, 415 or 606), the call
  retries once with plain RTP. `mandatory` allows SRTP only, and other offers get 488.
- `srtp_suites` may not be empty unless `srtp = "off"`. It also accepts `AES_256_CM_HMAC_SHA1_80` and `_32`.
- Use `tone:` or `silence` for headless runs.

<details>
<summary>How the SRTP fallback is remembered</summary>

After a plain retry succeeds, Siple remembers the refusal per destination (next hop `host:port[:transport]`, at most 64). Later
calls there offer plain RTP first. If that is refused, they retry once with SRTP and forget the destination when that succeeds.
`srtp_optional_downgraded` in `status` and `account.get` lists them. The list clears when `srtp`, `registrar`, `outbound_proxy`,
`domain`, `transport`, `codecs` or `srtp_suites` change, and on a rebuild. Some PBXes (FreeSWITCH without
`rtp_allow_crypto_in_avp=true`) ring the callee twice on the first call. Against those, use `srtp = "off"` or `"mandatory"`.

</details>

### Call behavior

| Key | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `dnd` | `false` | Do not disturb: reject incoming calls. Also settable with `account.set_dnd`. |
| `dnd_reject_code` | `486` | SIP status for DND rejects. 400 to 699. |
| `auto_answer` | `false` | Answer incoming calls automatically. |
| `answer_delay_ms` | `0` | Wait before auto-answering. At most 60000. |
| `allow_intercom` | `false` | Also auto-answer intercom and paging requests. |
| `auto_hold` | `true` | Answering or dialing holds your other calls. `false` also exempts this account. |
| `hold_style` | `"sendonly"` | Direction when you hold: `sendonly` or `inactive`. |
| `hold_send_cn` | `false` | Send comfort noise while holding. |
| `follow_redirects` | `true` | Follow 3xx replies on outgoing calls (max 3). |
| `max_calls` | `4` | Concurrent calls. More get 486. At least 1. |
| `accept_refer` | `true` | Accept incoming transfers (REFER). |
| `attended_refer_target` | `"aor"` | Address in `Refer-To` for attended transfers: `aor` or `contact`. |
| `attended_refer_on` | `"original"` | Call that carries the REFER: `original` or `consult`. |
| `auth_foreign` | `false` | Answer digest challenges from servers that are not your provider. |
| `prack` | `"supported"` | Reliable provisional responses: `disabled`, `supported` or `required`. |
| `session_timers` | `true` | RFC 4028 session timers. |
| `session_expires` | `1800` | Session lifetime requested, in seconds. 1 to 86400. |
| `min_se` | `90` | Shortest session interval accepted. 1 to 86400. |

- `hold_style = "sendonly"` becomes `inactive` if the other side already holds you.
- `auth_foreign = false` answers a challenge only when the request went to your provider (`outbound_proxy`, `registrar` or
  `domain`, or what they resolve to; with a separate `registrar`, the `domain` itself is not resolved). The request-URI, Contact and
  Record-Route are never trusted. Otherwise it fails with `auth_foreign_refused` and sends no credentials. Set `true` only
  if your provider uses other servers. A rogue host could then collect a crackable digest of your password.
- `prack`: `supported` sends reliable 180s only if the caller sends `Require: 100rel`. `required` does it whenever the caller
  lists `100rel`. `disabled` never does. The 200 then waits for PRACK, and the call is rejected with 504 if it never comes.
- `session_timers = false` ignores `Session-Expires` and answers `Require: timer` with 420. A peer that fails to refresh in time
  gets a BYE (cause 408). `session_expires` must be at least `min_se`. The RFC floor for `min_se` is 90.

### Voicemail light, extensions and tracing

| Key | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `mwi` | `"subscribe"` | Message-waiting light: `subscribe`, `unsolicited` or `off`. Resubscribes on change. |
| `mwi_uri` | `""` | URI to subscribe on. `""` is your own address. Resubscribes on change. |
| `blf_expires` | `600` | Extension subscription lifetime, in seconds. 10 to 86400. Resubscribes on change. |
| `pickup` | `"off"` | Directed pickup: `off`, `prefix` or `replaces` (experimental). |
| `pickup_prefix` | `"**"` | Feature code dialed before the extension with `prefix`. No spaces. |
| `pickup_replaces_target` | `"caller"` | With `replaces`: send to the `caller` or the `monitored` phone. |
| `sip_trace` | `true` | Write the wire-level SIP trace `<log folder>/sip/<id>/sip.<date>.log`. |

`mwi = "subscribe"` also accepts unsolicited NOTIFYs. Raise `blf_expires` when you watch many extensions. `**` is the FreePBX and
FreeSWITCH pickup code; Asterisk often uses `*8`. `account.set_sip_trace` overrides `sip_trace` until the file field changes.

### `[[account.blf]]`

The watch list of extensions (busy lamps). Put each table right after its account. Editing entries is hot, and only a changed
`uri` resubscribes. Guide: [See who is free](https://zyno-io.github.io/siple/guide/extensions).

| Key | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `uri` | required | Extension or full SIP URI to watch. Unique in the account. |
| `label` | `""` | Name shown in the window. |
| `group` | `""` | Group name. `""` groups by account. |
| `pickup` | `""` | Overrides the account's `pickup` (`off`, `prefix`, `replaces`). `""` inherits. |
| `pickup_prefix` | `""` | Overrides the account's `pickup_prefix`. `""` inherits. |
| `pickup_target` | `""` | With `prefix`: dial exactly this (queue codes, park slots). |

### Directed pickup

Pickup is offered only while the extension is ringing, its lamp is current and its method is not `off`. `prefix` dials
`pickup_target`, else `<pickup_prefix><extension>` (such as `**1004`). `replaces` is experimental. Every pickup goes through your
outbound proxy or registrar, and a target taken from a NOTIFY is cut down to `sip[s]:user@host[:port]` (parameters and headers
dropped). Full rules: [user guide](https://zyno-io.github.io/siple/guide/extensions#directed-pickup-rules).

```toml
[[account]]
pickup = "prefix"
pickup_prefix = "**"

[[account.blf]]
uri = "1004"
label = "Support queue"
```

### BLF subscription health

`blf.list` and `status` report each entry's `subscription` (`phase`, `last_code`, `last_problem`, `expires_at`, `retry_at`) and a
`stale` flag. `phase` is `pending`, `active`, `retrying` or `failed`. Details:
[user guide](https://zyno-io.github.io/siple/guide/extensions#blf-subscription-health).

### Passwords in the system keyring

`password_store = "keyring"` keeps the password in the macOS Keychain, Windows Credential Manager or Linux Secret Service instead
of `config.toml`. With no credential store or no entry, that account is switched off with an error. Siple never falls back to a
plaintext password. Details: [user guide](https://zyno-io.github.io/siple/guide/configuration#passwords-in-the-system-keyring).

## Vendor profiles

`profile = "yealink" | "polycom" | "cisco" | "grandstream"` makes an account act like a desk-phone family by filling in defaults.
**Precedence: a key in the file, then the profile, then the built-in default.** An empty `user_agent` or `dtmf_mode` counts as not
set. `config.get` shows the resolved values. The bundles approximate factory behavior and are not certified.

| Key | `generic` | `yealink` | `polycom` | `cisco` | `grandstream` |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `user_agent` | `[general]` value | `Yealink SIP-T54W 96.86.0.70` | `PolycomVVX-VVX_450-UA/6.4.4.5239` | `Cisco-CP-8845-3PCC/12.0.1` | `Grandstream GXP2170 1.0.11.74` |
| `hold_style` | `sendonly` | `sendonly` | `sendonly` | `inactive` | `sendonly` |
| `dtmf_mode` | inherits `[media]` (`auto`) | `rfc4733` | `rfc4733` | `rfc4733` | `rfc4733` |
| `dnd_reject_code` | `486` | `480` | `486` | `480` | `486` |
| `attended_refer_target` | `aor` | `aor` | `contact` | `contact` | `aor` |
| `prack` | `supported` | `disabled` | `supported` | `supported` | `disabled` |
| `session_timers` | `true` | `false` | `true` | `true` | `true` |

## When a file is rejected

Nothing is applied if the file is not valid TOML or has unknown keys; has empty, malformed or duplicate account ids; has an enabled
account without `username` or `domain`; breaks a range above; has a bad `tls_fingerprint`; sets `password` with
`password_store = "keyring"`; sets `session_expires` below `min_se`; sets only one of `tls_cert` and `tls_key`; sets
`control.tcp_listen` to a non-loopback address; has an invalid `log_level`; or has a bad `[ui.shortcuts]` or `[speech]` entry.

## Files next to the config

| File | What it is |
|---|---|
| `state.toml` | App state that is not a setting, such as `first_run_completed`. Safe to delete. |
| `runtime.json` | Mode 0600: pid, control endpoints and the per-run token. Removed on clean shutdown. |
| `siple.lock` | Single-instance lock for this config folder. |
| `control.sock` | Control socket (macOS, Linux), unless overridden. |
| `logs/siple.<date>.log`, `logs/sip/<id>/sip.<date>.log` | Application log and per-account SIP traces, rolled daily. |
